# Reporting — {{project.name}}

This project ships **two applications**, and this directory is the second one.

```
docker compose up --build
```

| | |
|---|---|
| http://localhost:{{project.frontendPort}} | **{{project.name}}** — the application |
| http://localhost:3100 | **Enterprise Reporting** — its reports, charts and dashboard |

The first start migrates and seeds the application, then loads its schema and
the reporting pack into the platform. Until `report-seeder` exits, the reporting
application is up but **empty**; `docker compose logs -f report-seeder` shows it
working.

## Two applications, two logins, and why they are not merged

This is the thing most likely to be got wrong, so it is stated here rather than
left to be discovered.

|  | {{project.name}} | Enterprise Reporting |
|---|---|---|
| Port | {{project.frontendPort}} | 3100 |
| Database | `{{project.name | replace '-' '_'}}` | `enterprise_config` |
| Users | its own table, its own session | its own table, its own session |
| A role decides | what you may **do** to a record | which tables your queries may **read** |
| Password | `{{reporting.appPassword}}` | `{{reporting.reportPassword}}` |

**The role names line up; nothing else is shared.** Both sides are derived from
the same `%%rbac` directives in the model this project was generated from, so
`sales_manager` is recognisable on either side — but they are separate accounts
in separate databases, nothing is federated, and neither password works on the
other side. Merging them would force one product's meaning of "role" onto the
other.

The addresses differ on purpose. Identical ones would invite you to try a single
password on both.

### The accounts

{{reporting.roles}} roles, one account each side. "Tables" is what the reporting
role may read — which is the whole of what signing in as it does.

| Role | Application account | Reporting account | Tables |
|---|---|---|---|
{{reporting.accounts}}

The administrator is the exception and keeps the same address on both sides,
because that is what the reporting platform bootstraps for itself. It is still
two different accounts in two different databases.

**Only `read` rules narrow a reporting role.** `%%rbac` also restricts create,
update and delete, and none of that means anything to somebody who cannot write
through the reporting platform at all.

## What is in the pack

`reporting-pack.json` is the whole of this project's reporting layer:
**{{reporting.queries}} saved queries, {{reporting.reports}} reports,
{{reporting.charts}} charts and {{reporting.dashboards}} dashboard**. Nothing in
it is invented — every query is derived from something the model declares:

| The model declares | The pack gets |
|---|---|
| an entity | a register — what rows exist, newest first |
| an `%%enum`-bound column | a breakdown, as a chart |
| `created_at` | volume by month, as a line |
| a `kind: state` workflow | a lifecycle over the states the diagram declares, in its order, zeroes included |
| numeric columns | a measures report, grouped by the entity's primary enum column |
| `oneToMany` | children per parent, ranked |
| `%%report` | the question its author wrote, listed first and at the top of the dashboard |

Names and descriptions come from `%%entity help:` and `%%field help:` — the only
place a model says what an entity is *for* rather than what shape it is. That is
the difference between a report called "bus_account by status" and one called
"Accounts by status" that explains what an account is in this business.

Edit the pack and re-run the seeder (`docker compose up report-seeder`) to
change what the platform holds; it upserts by name, so a second run updates in
place rather than duplicating.

## How the platform gets here

`Dockerfile` fetches the platform's source at build time and builds it. It is a
separate product — around ninety megabytes of TanStack Start over PostgreSQL —
so it is **not vendored into this project**: the only reporting artefact this
repository contains is the pack.

That means `docker build` needs network access. On a build host without it,
clone the repository yourself and point the `report` service's `context` at the
clone in `docker-compose.yml`; the build is otherwise identical.

`REPORT_REF` chooses the version, and defaults to `main`. Pin it in `.env` once
this project is something you deploy rather than something you are looking at:

```sh
REPORT_REF=<commit-sha>
```

## Configuration

Set these in the `.env` beside `docker-compose.yml`, **before the first start**:

```sh
REPORT_AUTH_SECRET=         # at least 32 characters
REPORT_ENCRYPTION_KEY=      # exactly 64 hex characters
REPORT_PORT=3100            # where the reporting application is published
REPORT_REF=main             # the platform version to build
```

**`REPORT_ENCRYPTION_KEY` has to stay the same.** It is what the platform
encrypts the stored data-source password with, so regenerating it leaves every
connection undecryptable — and the failure looks like a broken data source
rather than a rotated key.

Generate both:

```sh
openssl rand -hex 32   # REPORT_ENCRYPTION_KEY (64 hex characters)
openssl rand -base64 32  # REPORT_AUTH_SECRET
```

## Running without it

The reporting application is part of `docker compose up`. To leave it out, name
the services you do want:

```sh
docker compose up postgres electric backend frontend
```

Nothing in the application depends on it — it reads the application's database
and never writes to it.
